Start();
register(SoT);
define
    soRq form="firstLight";
    soWalk code={
        for grup in argument;
            sn = string $*grup.taG$; sa := *grup.isAttributE;
            cerr "ENTRY" sn sa:;
        };
    soList code={
        k := searchSave(0);
        soWalk(*k);
        };
    ;
show(soRq);
search SoT;
showStage();
showSearch();
cerr "SO PAGE 1":;   soList();
search cOLOr;
cerr "SO PAGE 2":;   soList();
search Utilities dESCRIPTIONs SoT;
cerr "SO PAGE 3":;   soList();
search reset Grokking SoT sTYLEs cOLOr;
cerr "SO PAGE 4":;   soList();
search sTYLEs fONTs;
cerr "SO PAGE 5":;   soList();
cerr "SO SENTINEL":;
stop();

searchOnceT -- a registry already on the search list keeps its place (Tony, 2026-11-27; SEQ 790).

What it asks. The search list's order is its meaning: the first registry holding a name answers. Before this
stroke, show's stage step and its search step each appended their registries, and a plain search appended a
registry that was already there, so the list carried repeats (cOLOr fONTs sTYLEs twice during every show).
Now show adds its registries as members, and both show and search skip a registry already on the list: no
second entry, and the first one does not move. Changing the order is done by search reset and naming everything.

The pages. Each prints the list as ENTRY name affiliation, one line per entry (affiliation 0 is a member).
    1   after show, search SoT, then show's own two steps called by name (showStage, showSearch): show puts
        the list back when it ends, so its steps are called here to leave their adds on the list.
    2   search cOLOr, already there: the list is page 1's.
    3   search Utilities dESCRIPTIONs SoT: two already there and one new. Only the new one is added, at the end.
        This is the row that fails if search stopped adding anything at all.
    4   search reset, then four names: exactly those four, in that order.
    5   after the reset, search sTYLEs fONTs: sTYLEs stays where it was, fONTs is added at the end.
